{"card":{"card_id":"ma-c-58b24c5737b5684cb415","statement":"Being the category of categories, $\\lincat_\\La$ admits $2$-categorical structures. On the other hand, as mentioned in <<ref:rem-generalization-corr>>, the category of correspondences also admits a $2$-categorical enhancement. Sheaf theory constructed in <<ref:prop-sheaf-theory-for-adjoint-factorization>> can be enhanced at the $2$-categorical level as a functor\n\n\\[\n\\corr(\\bfC)_{\\verti';\\horiz}^{\\mathrm{E}}\\to \\lincat_\\La,\n\\]\n\nat least if all morphisms in $\\mathrm{E}$ are $m$-truncated for some $-2\\leq m<\\infty$, by applying <<cite:Gaitsgory.Rozenblyum.DAG.vol.I>> (together with <<cite:liu2012enhanceda>>) inductively to the pair $\\mathrm{E}_{i-1}\\subset \\mathrm{E}_i$, where $\\mathrm{E}_i\\subset\\mathrm{E}$ is the subclass of those morphisms that are $i$-truncated.\n\n- Suppose we have the sheaf theory constructed in <<ref:prop-sheaf-theory-for-adjoint-factorization>>. Let $X_\\bullet$ be a Segal object in $\\bfC$ as in <<ref:rem-conv-product>>. If all morphisms of the simplicial objects $X_\\bullet$ are in $\\bfC_{\\mathrm{HL}}$, then $\\La_{X_1}\\in \\der(X_1)$ is a natural algebra object with respect to the convolution monoidal structure of $\\der(X_1)$. Indeed, the multiplication of $\\La_{X_1}$ amounts to a morphism\n\n\\[\n(d_0\\times d_2)\\horizl(\\La_{X_1}\\boxtimes_{\\La} \\La_{X_1})\\cong \\La_{X_2} \\to (d_2)\\vertr(\\La_{X_1}),\n\\]\n\nwhich is given by the adjunction $(d_2)\\vertl((d_2)\\horizl(\\La_{X_1}))=((d_2)\\horizl)^L((d_2)\\horizl(\\La_{X_1}))\\to \\La_{X_1}$.\n\n- There are variants of the above theorem. E.g. Instead of assuming that each $f\\in \\verti'$ admits a decomposition as in the theorem, one could assume that each $f$ admits a decomposition $f=f_{\\mathrm{E}}\\circ f_{\\verti}$. One could also replace $\\mathrm{E}\\subset \\mathrm{HL}$ by $\\mathrm{E}\\subset \\mathrm{HR}$, $\\mathrm{E}\\subset \\mathrm{VL}$ or $\\mathrm{E}\\subset \\mathrm{VR}$ and the corresponding assumptions. (In the case $\\der$ takes value in $\\cptcat_\\La$, one further requires the right adjoints of morphisms in $\\mathrm{E}\\subset \\mathrm{HR}$ and in $\\mathrm{E}\\subset \\mathrm{VR}$ preserve compact objects.) The proofs remain the same.\n\n- Note that in fact in the statement of <<ref:prop-sheaf-theory-for-adjoint-factorization>> one may replace $\\lincat_\\La$ by $\\cat$ or other symmetric monoidal $2$-category. The proof does not change.","source_id":"arXiv:2504.07482","cluster":"representation_theory","statement_sha256":"1dc60a7dbd89f540298a2f9694605c3c66f9c0283601d8af8d01a68caa31d76f","kind":"remark","label":"Remark 582","ref_key":"rem-sheaf-theory-for-adjoint-factorization","name":"","order":826,"deps":[{"ref":"prop-sheaf-theory-for-adjoint-factorization","card_id":"ma-c-98e2525d020dbef189ef","label":"Theorem 581","resolved":true,"target":"card"},{"ref":"rem-conv-product","card_id":"ma-c-500fd66cb41164239848","label":"Remark 560","resolved":true,"target":"card"},{"ref":"rem-generalization-corr","card_id":"ma-c-b1aa2ce71484743bc724","label":"Remark 542","resolved":true,"target":"card"}],"cite_keys":["Gaitsgory.Rozenblyum.DAG.vol.I","liu2012enhanceda"],"provenance":{"source_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.07482","harvest_mode":"arxiv_eprint","payload_sha256":"1b700e8c4a2fc6f0c0f522039f223820ba6737a9a0ac91aea37c58ba7a3389c2","line":15915,"origin":"latex_env","retrieved_at":"2026-09-28T11:10:54+08:00"},"wash":{"version":"cardwash.v3","stages":["segment","purify","classify","dedupe","emit"],"numbering":"document_order_with_label_heuristic","detail":{"stripped_proof":false,"chars":2316,"math_hints":160}}},"paper":{"source_id":"arXiv:2504.07482","title":"Tame categorical local Langlands correspondence","abstract":"In one of our previous articles, we outlined the formulation of a version of the categorical arithmetic local Langlands conjecture. The aims of this article are threefold. First, we provide a detailed account of one component of this conjecture: the local Langlands category. Second, we aim to prove this conjecture in the tame case for quasi-split unramified reductive groups. Finally, we will explore the first applications of such categorical equivalence.","authors":["Xinwen Zhu"],"year":"2025","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.07482","cluster":"representation_theory","macros":{"fraka":"{\\mathfrak a}","frakb":"{\\mathfrak b}","frakc":"{\\mathfrak c}","frakg":"{\\mathfrak g}","frakh":"{\\mathfrak h}","frakm":"{\\mathfrak m}","frakp":"{\\mathfrak p}","fraks":"{\\mathfrak s}","frakt":"{\\mathfrak t}","fraku":"{\\mathfrak u}","frakA":"{\\mathfrak A}","frakC":"{\\mathfrak C}","frakM":"{\\mathfrak M}","frakX":"{\\mathfrak X}","bA":"{\\mathbb A}","bB":"{\\mathbb B}","bC":"{\\mathbb C}","bD":"{\\mathbb D}","bF":"{\\mathbb F}","bG":"{\\mathbb G}","bL":"{\\mathbb L}","bN":"{\\mathbb N}","bO":"{\\mathbb O}","bP":"{\\mathbb P}","bQ":"{\\mathbb Q}","bR":"{\\mathbb R}","bT":"{\\mathbb T}","bX":"{\\mathbb X}","bZ":"{\\mathbb Z}","bfA":"{\\mathbf A}","bfB":"{\\mathbf B}","bfC":"{\\mathbf C}","bfD":"{\\mathbf D}","bfE":"{\\mathbf E}","bfF":"{\\mathbf F}","bfG":"{\\mathbf G}","bfH":"{\\mathbf H}","bfI":"{\\mathbf I}","bfK":"{\\mathbf K}","bfL":"{\\mathbf L}","bfM":"{\\mathbf M}","bfN":"{\\mathbf N}","bfP":"{\\mathbf P}","bfR":"{\\mathbf R}","bfS":"{\\mathbf S}","bfT":"{\\mathbf T}","bfa":"{\\mathbf a}","bff":"{\\mathbf f}","mA":"{\\mathcal A}","mB":"{\\mathcal B}","mE":"{\\mathcal E}","mF":"{\\mathcal F}","mG":"{\\mathcal G}","mH":"{\\mathcal H}","mI":"{\\mathcal I}","mJ":"{\\mathcal J}","mK":"{\\mathcal K}","mL":"{\\mathcal L}","mM":"{\\mathcal M}","mN":"{\\mathcal N}","mO":"{\\mathcal O}","mP":"{\\mathcal P}","mQ":"{\\mathcal Q}","mS":"{\\mathcal S}","mT":"{\\mathcal T}","mU":"{\\mathcal U}","mV":"{\\mathcal V}","mW":"{\\mathcal W}","mX":"{\\mathcal X}","mZ":"{\\mathcal Z}","scrA":"{\\mathscr A}","scrB":"{\\mathscr B}","scrS":"{\\mathscr S}","al":"{\\alpha}","ga":"{\\gamma}","Ga":"{\\Gamma}","la":"{\\l